<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Template package for `dotnet new` Tizen MAUI projects.

    Template packs carry content, not compiled output, so this project deliberately does
    not participate in the Tizen workload gate: it can be packed on any machine, including
    ones where the emitted project cannot yet be restored.

    The emitted project targets net11.0-tizen11.0, matching Directory.Build.props and
    eng/baselines.json. It does NOT PackageReference the TizenFX reference pack: that is a
    DotnetPlatform-typed package delivered by the Samsung workload, and referencing it directly
    fails restore with NU1213. Until the Samsung .NET 11 workload ships, a generated project
    will hit the same external gate every Tizen project in this repository hits; that is the
    honest shape, and it is why the template pins the versioned TFM rather than an unversioned
    net11.0-tizen.

    NO C# PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES IN TEMPLATE CONTENT.

    The .NET Template Engine treats `#if` / `#else` / `#endif` in template content as TEMPLATE
    conditionals rather than compiler directives, and it recognizes them even when they are
    commented out (the "commented conditional" form). Two distinct failures came from this:

      * MauiProgram.cs guarded the Tizen host-builder call with `#if TIZEN`. TIZEN is not a
        template symbol, so the engine evaluated the block as false and emitted only the
        else-branch. Every generated application silently called UseMauiApp instead of
        UseMauiAppTizen, with the Microsoft.Maui.Platforms.Tizen.Hosting `using` stripped out
        alongside it. The file read correctly here and was wrong in every instantiation.

      * Explaining the above INSIDE the template file reintroduced it: a commented-out example
        of the directives is still parsed, and `dotnet new` failed outright with
        "Index was out of range" and a half-written project directory.

    So the rationale lives here, in the packaging project, which is not template content. The
    emitted project has exactly one target framework, so there is no branch to select; if it
    ever grows more, use a template symbol and a real MSBuild condition.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsTizenProject>false</IsTizenProject>
    <PackageType>Template</PackageType>
    <PackageId>Maui.Tizen.Templates</PackageId>
    <Description>Project templates for building .NET MAUI applications for Tizen.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);templates;dotnet-new</PackageTags>

    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
    <NoDefaultExcludes>true</NoDefaultExcludes>
    <!--
      Nothing under templates/ is a project item. The files there carry unresolved template
      placeholders (the project file's TFM is literally net11.0-tizenTIZEN_PLATFORM_VERSION), so
      they must be packed as content and never globbed into this project's own build.
    -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <ProduceReferenceAssembly>false</ProduceReferenceAssembly>
    <!-- No lib/ output, so there is nothing for a symbol package to carry. -->
    <IncludeSymbols>false</IncludeSymbols>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      PackagePath is written out per file rather than left to $(ContentTargetFolders).

      A template package is only a template package if the directory tree survives packing:
      `dotnet new install <nupkg>` looks for a `.template.config/template.json` and then treats
      the directory CONTAINING it as the template source root. Flatten the tree and the package
      still installs - it just emits the package's own internals into the user's folder instead
      of a project.

      Relying on $(ContentTargetFolders) makes that tree an inference: NuGet reconstructs the
      relative path from the item's identity, and any Content item it cannot relate back to the
      project directory (an absolute include, a linked file, a Content item contributed by
      another import) is written flat into the target folder instead. Writing the destination
      explicitly removes the inference, so the layout cannot depend on how the item was
      specified or on which NuGet version does the packing.

      Pinned end to end by PackageContentTests: the produced nupkg is installed into an isolated
      template hive and instantiated, so a flattened package fails the tests rather than the
      first person to run `dotnet new`.
    -->
    <Content Include="templates/**/*"
             Exclude="templates/**/bin/**;templates/**/obj/**"
             Pack="true"
             PackagePath="content/templates/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
